add(H1('Abstract'));
add(P('Copper is a trace element, considered an inorganic contaminant when found at high concentrations. This work aimed to study the role of the arbuscular mycorrhizal (AM) fungus *Rhizophagus intraradices* (GC3 strain) in the immobilization of Cu, through in vitro (bi-compartmental Petri plates with root organ culture) and in vivo (sunflower greenhouse experiment) assays. We used X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and micro-particle-induced X-ray emission (microPIXE) to understand Cu distribution in fungal structures, and we measured glomalin-related soil protein (GRSP) production and its relation with soil aggregation at different Cu concentrations. {{c:claimAb}}==GRSP production by GC3 increased with the Cu concentration. This response to the presence of the pollutant makes *R. intraradices* strain GC3 a good candidate for future bioremediation projects.=={{/c}}'));
add(PL('**Keywords:** {{c:c0}}trace element{{/c}}; copper; *Rhizophagus intraradices*; soil aggregation; glomalin'));

add(H1('{{c:c1}}Introduction{{/c}}'));
add(P('Copper (Cu) is a {{c:term}}trace element (TE){{/c}} essential for humans, animals, plants and microorganisms; it participates in various morphological, physiological and biochemical processes. Usually, 5–30 mg kg^−1^ of Cu in crop tissues is regarded as adequate (Adriano 2001). Cu also meets the definition of a heavy metal (HM) according to its density (8.96 g cm^−3^) and atomic number (29) (Linder and Hazegh-Azam 1996). The edaphic content of Cu depends on complex interactions among parent material, physico-chemical properties and exogenous inputs from agriculture or industry (Ballabio et al. 2018). Excess Cu in soil can be toxic for plants; however, soil P availability seems to insolubilize Cu, improve plant nutrition and mitigate the negative effects of Cu toxicity (Baldi et al. 2018). Soil contamination by Cu arises from multiple sources, including fertilizer production, mining, excessive application of pesticides and fungicides, industrial processes and irrigation with wastewater, among others (Bremner 1998; {{c:cites}}==Liu and Beckerman 2022==){{/c}}. Because HMs are non-biodegradable and extensively used, they tend to accumulate in the environment, posing significant toxic and carcinogenic risks to human health (==Collins et al. 2014; Wang 2023; Jeremias et al. 2023; Alshammari et al. 2023==).'));
add(P('{{c:c2}}Unlike organic compounds, they require techniques that involve the immobilization, transformation and/or removal of the contaminant to ensure successful soil restoration and remediation.{{/c}} Many of the existing engineering technologies are highly expensive, and soil-disturbing practices hinder the treatment of large areas (Lasat 2002). Phytoremediation is an alternative that overcomes these limitations by taking advantage of plant responses to inorganic pollutants. {{c:hyper}}==Plants used for phytoextraction of HMs are hyperaccumulators and have large biomass, such as sunflower (*Helianthus annuus*)== (==Sytar et al. 2021==){{/c}}. It is an indicator plant, and its morphological characteristics depend on various ecological factors, including HM exposure (Murillo et al. 1999; ==Khalilvand-Behrouzyar et al. 2007==). The hyperaccumulating capacity of these plant species can be enhanced by mutualistic association with AM fungi (phylum Glomeromycota) (Schüßler et al. 2001). These root symbionts improve nutritional status and promote growth through an extensive extraradical mycelium (ERM). The ERM acts as a prolongation of roots, allowing better exploration of soil and increasing uptake of water and nutrients, mainly phosphates and micronutrients (Parniske 2008); it also contributes to the formation and stabilization of soil aggregates (Rillig and Mummey 2006).'));
add(P('AM fungi can capture and adsorb HMs in soil within fungal structures and alleviate the toxicity of these elements for plants (Hildebrandt et al. 2007; Janeeshma and Puthur 2020; {{c:c3}}Son et al. 2024{{/c}}). They synthesize glomalin-related soil protein (GRSP), a recalcitrant glycoprotein related to long-term carbon sequestration, soil aggregation, soil remediation and soil fertility. Their hyphal walls form complexes with potentially toxic elements and inactivate {{c:c4}}them in the mycorrhizosphere{{/c}} (Rillig 2004; Nichols and Wright 2006; González-Chávez et al. 2004). {{c:c5}}{{c:stAtt}}==Statello et al. (2024)== have demonstrated that GRSP from polluted soils sequesters and stabilizes HM ions, thus reducing their bioavailability and toxicity for microorganisms and plants.{{/c}}{{/c}} Cornejo et al. (2013) suggested that a fungal strategy to survive in Cu-polluted environments is to compartmentalize excess Cu in spores, and Statello et al. (2024), using microPIXE in structures of the *Rhizophagus intraradices* GC3 strain associated with Ri T-DNA carrot roots, showed significant Cu accumulation, suggesting a potential mechanism of toxicity alleviation in the host plant.'));
add(P('{{c:c6}}Nowadays, various HM detection techniques allow a better understanding of the uptake, translocation and localization of HMs in biological matrices, including mycorrhizal roots and fungal structures. In particular, microPIXE and XRF are adequate technologies for the qualitative and quantitative analysis of material composition (Custo et al. 2005; de la Fournière et al. 2019).{{/c}} Thus, the aim of this work was to investigate the uptake, translocation and localization of Cu and P in mycorrhizal sunflower plants {{c:c8}}and fungal structures{{/c}} under different culture conditions using both detection technologies, and how copper stress affects GRSP production by the studied strain.'));

add(H1('Materials and methods'));
add(H2('{{c:c15}}{{c:c16}}Arbuscular mycorrhizal inoculum and plant material{{/c}}{{/c}}'));
add(P('The AM fungus *Rhizophagus intraradices* (N.C. Schenck & G.S. Sm.) C. Walker & A. Schüßler (GC3 strain) was studied using different growing systems: in vivo, using sunflower (*Helianthus annuus* L. var. Mammoth Russian; {{c:c12}}==[seed size]=={{/c}}) as host because {{c:c11}}{{c:c10}}it is considered a model plant{{/c}}{{/c}} (Castañón-Silva et al. 2013; ==Francis and Ndukwn 2019==; Scotti et al. 2019), and in vitro, an axenic system using Ri T-DNA transformed carrot roots (TCR; Silvani et al. 2019). The *R. intraradices* strain was provided by the Banco de Glomeromycota In Vitro (BGIV, www.bgiv.com.ar) and replicated in association with TCR in solid Minimal Medium (MM; Bécard and Fortin 1988).'));
add(H2('In vivo assay'));
add(P('Sunflower seeds were surface-sterilized with 70% (v/v) ethanol for 20 min and 20% (v/v) sodium hypochlorite plus Tween 20 (0.1%) for 30 min, rinsed with sterile distilled water and placed on moist filter paper (Whatman #1, Maidstone, UK) at 25 °C in the dark for 4 days. Three germinated seeds were sown in 1.5-L pots with tyndallized soil (see Annex 1). Three days after emergence, two of the three seedlings were removed, selecting a homogeneous set of plants, and inoculation with GC3 (where appropriate) was immediately carried out by placing a 1-cm^3^ plug of 4-month-old monoxenic culture (containing mycelium, spores and fragments of colonized roots). Non-mycorrhizal control plants received a 1-cm^3^ plug of MM with TCR only.'));
add(P('Pots were watered by capillarity with deionized water and fertilized every 15 days with 100 mL of Hewitt solution without phosphorus (Hewitt 1952). After thirty days, the soil in each pot was amended with {{c:dose}}420 mL of CuSO~4~ solution (Cu1: 150 mg L^−1^; Cu2: 300 mg L^−1^) or 420 mL of sterile deionized water (Cu0){{/c}}. The experiment used a randomized design in a greenhouse with natural light and temperature for three months. Twenty-four experimental units were divided into six treatments (n = 4): GC3-Cu0, GC3-Cu1, GC3-Cu2, W/I-Cu0, W/I-Cu1 and W/I-Cu2, where W/I denotes plants without AM fungal inoculation.'));
add(H2('In vitro assay using transformed carrot roots as a model'));
add(P('The in vitro assay was performed as described in Silvani et al. (2019), using two-segmented plates to separate a root compartment (RC) from a hyphal compartment (HC; St-Arnaud et al. 1996). The RC was filled with 30 mL of MM and a non-mycorrhizal TCR fragment (5 cm long) and was inoculated with a plug of 4-month-old monoxenic culture containing approximately 200 spores, mycelium and colonized roots of *R. intraradices* GC3. The HC was filled with 30 mL of sterile dry soil (the same as above) and homogeneously amended with 4 mL of filter-sterilized (Millipore, 0.2 µm pore size) CuSO~4~ solution (Cu1: 150 mg L^−1^; Cu2: 300 mg L^−1^) or 4 mL of sterile deionized water (Cu0). The Cu1 concentration corresponded to values recorded in soil samples from the Riachuelo basin (Avellaneda, Buenos Aires, 34° 39′ 27.36″ S, 58° 22′ 34.37″ W; Colombo et al. 2020). Non-mycorrhizal cultures (W/I) ==[were inoculated? prepared?]== with a root fragment (5 cm long) placed in the RC. Three replicates were prepared for each treatment and incubated in the dark at 25 °C. To assess growth of {{c:c13}}carrot roots{{/c}} in the RC, root length was measured in five randomly selected 1-cm^2^ squares using the formula of Newman (1966).'));
add(H2('Harvest'));
add(P('At the end of the in vivo experiment (130 days), shoots and roots were harvested separately. Dry weight of shoots and roots was recorded after 48 h of oven-drying at 70 °C. Subsamples of air-dried roots were stained to determine AM fungal root colonization, and Cu concentrations were quantified in shoot and root dry mass. ==[In vitro harvest: complete.]=='));
add(H2('Estimation of soil aggregation by AM inoculation'));
add(P('At harvest, a portion of the root system was dried for 48 h at 25 °C. The quantity of soil adhering to roots after inoculation was assessed according to Graham et al. (1982). Roots were vigorously shaken to remove soil not firmly attached, and the remaining particles were removed by soaking in distilled water. Washed roots were weighed fresh and dry, and the soil suspension was decanted and dried to constant weight ==[temperature]==. The amount of soil was expressed as grams of dry soil per gram of fresh root.'));
add(H2('ERM length and spore number'));
add(P('One gram of dry rhizospheric soil was sampled from each pot and from the HC. Samples were placed in Erlenmeyer flasks with 100 mL of 0.002% trypan blue and vigorously shaken for 10 min. Aliquots of the soil suspension were poured into a 9-cm Petri dish with a 1-cm^2^ counting grid. Intersections of blue-stained AM fungal hyphae with grid lines were quantified using the formula of Newman (1966) to determine centimetres of ERM per gram of dry soil. ERM length in the RC was assessed by counting five randomly selected 1-cm^2^ squares and applying the same calculation. Spore production in the RC was estimated by adding the spores counted in five randomly located 1-cm^2^ squares in each plate.'));
add(H2('AM root colonization'));
add(P('Roots from in vivo (130 days) and in vitro (120 days) assays were collected to measure AM colonization. Samples were cleared (10% w/v KOH, 90 °C, 15 min), acidified with 0.1 N HCl (5 min) and stained with trypan blue in lactic acid (0.02% w/v; 90 °C, 10 min; modified from Phillips and Hayman 1970). The frequency (%F) and intensity (%I) of colonization were measured according to Declerck et al. (2004). Fifty randomly selected root pieces were mounted on microscope slides in groups of 10 and examined under a Nikon Optiphot-2 microscope at 400×; arbuscules (%A), vesicles (%V) and intraradical spores (%IS) were recorded. The number of newly formed spores per gram of dry soil was counted under a stereomicroscope (Olympus SZ61) at 4.5×.'));
add(H2('Glomalin-related soil protein (GRSP) analysis'));
add(P('GRSP was determined according to Cornejo et al. (2008). Easily extractable GRSP (EE-GRSP) was extracted from 1 g of dry soil with 8 mL of 20 mM citrate (pH 7.0) and autoclaved at 121 °C for 30 min. Total GRSP (T-GRSP) was obtained by repeated extractions from 1 g of dry soil with 8 mL of {{c:c14}}50 mM citrate buffer (pH 8.0){{/c}} and autoclaved at 121 °C for 60 min. In both cases, after cooling to 4 °C, the supernatant was separated by centrifugation (3000 rpm, 15 min). For T-GRSP, extractions were repeated five times on the same sample until the reddish-brown colour disappeared from the supernatants. Extracts from each cycle were pooled, centrifuged at 10,000 rpm for 10 min and analysed. Protein content in the supernatant was determined by the Bradford method (Sigma-Aldrich Inc.) with bovine serum albumin as the standard.'));
add(H2('Chemical element localization and quantification'));
add(P('{{c:c17}}{{c:c18}}{{c:c19}}MicroPIXE was employed{{/c}}{{/c}}{{/c}} to analyse the elemental composition of TCR and AM fungal structures in the in vitro assay. Analyses were conducted at CAC-CNEA following de la Fournière et al. (2019). Transverse root sections, 10 µm thick, were prepared using a cryo-microtome at −20 °C and transferred onto ultrapure polypropylene films with acrylic-glass target frames, where they were preserved until irradiation. To identify AM fungal colonization and relate it to elemental distributions, contiguous sections were stained with trypan blue (0.02% v/v) and observed at 400×. External spores and hyphae from the HC were isolated under a microscope and rinsed with Milli-Q water to remove soil particles; fungal vesicles were extracted from the TCR. All AM fungal structures were maintained in Milli-Q water, transferred to ultrapure polypropylene with acrylic-glass frames, dried and preserved until irradiation with a 50 MeV {{c:c20}}^16^O^5+^ ion beam{{/c}} focused to a spot size below 5 µm, scanning over a 256 × 256 pixel matrix. Spectra were analysed with OMDAQ-2007 by calculating the peak area using a Gaussian fit or the sum of counts per channel under the peak.'));
add(P('P and Cu concentrations in sunflower plants and soil samples from both assays were determined by XRF. Soil samples were dried at 70 °C for 48 h, milled and sieved through an 850-µm mesh to obtain a fine, homogeneous powder. Plant material (shoots and roots) was ground and sieved through the same mesh. The powder was pressed into pellets using a die and hydraulic press and immediately analysed according to the protocol of the Laboratorio de Fluorescencia de Rayos X, CAC-CNEA (Custo et al. 2005). The bioconcentration factor was calculated as the element concentration in shoot or root divided by its concentration in the substrate (BCF > 1 indicates potential bioaccumulation), and the translocation factor as the concentration in shoot divided by the concentration in root (TF > 1 indicates an ability to translocate the element from roots to shoots, primarily responsible for phytoextraction; Scotti et al. 2019).'));
add(H2('{{c:c21}}Data analysis{{/c}}'));
add(P('{{c:stat}}Analyses were performed in R 4.6.1{{/c}} (R Core Team 2026). Responses were analysed with generalized linear models (Gamma distribution, log link). Variables measured in inoculated and W/I units were analysed with inoculation, Cu level and their interaction as factors, using type II F tests (Fox and Weisberg 2019); GC3 and W/I were compared within each Cu level from estimated marginal means (Lenth 2025). Variables measured only in inoculated units were analysed with Cu level as the factor, followed by pairwise Tukey comparisons. Model fit was checked with simulated residuals (Hartig 2025). Correlations among variables were calculated as Pearson coefficients on complete cases. Significance was set at *P* < 0.05. Values are shown as mean ± standard error (SE).'));

add(H1('Results'));
add(H2('In vitro assay'));
add(P('The AM fungus *R. intraradices* strain GC3 developed in both RC and HC when exposed to Cu-contaminated soil (Figs. 1, 2). Hyphal length in the HC ranged from 70.0 ± 11.6 to 153.3 ± 24.5 cm g^−1^ and was higher in Cu1 than in Cu2 (*P* = 0.026), whereas Cu did not significantly affect hyphal length in the {{c:rc}}RC{{/c}} (*P* = 0.173; Fig. 2A, B). Spores in the HC differed among all Cu levels (141 ± 21, 445 ± 145 and 9 ± 2 g^−1^ in Cu0, Cu1 and Cu2; *P* < 0.001), and spores in the RC were more numerous in Cu2 than in Cu1 (397 ± 109 vs 89 ± 4; *P* = 0.008; {{c:c23}}Fig. 2C, D{{/c}}; Table S1). {{c:c24}}Cu2 mycelium showed particle agglomeration, unlike mycelium without Cu (Fig. 1D).{{/c}} Transformed root length ranged from 402 to 688 cm, with no significant differences among Cu levels (*P* = 0.536; Table S1).'));
add(IMG('Fig1_sistema_in_vitro.jpg', 410, 410, 'jpg'));
add(CAP('{{c:c41}}**Figure 1.**{{/c}} In vitro system with *Rhizophagus intraradices* GC3 and Ri T-DNA carrot roots. **A** Bi-compartmental Petri plate. **B** Mycelium growing in Cu1-treated soil. **C** Spores from Cu1. **D** Soil attached to the mycelium in Cu2. Scale bars: A, 1.5 cm; B and C, 400 µm; D, 50 µm.'));
add(IMG('Fig2_micelio_esporas_in_vitro.png', 491, 423));
add(CAP(`**Figure 2.** Hyphal length (**A**, **B**) and spore number (**C**, **D**) of *Rhizophagus intraradices* GC3 in the root compartment (RC) and the hyphal compartment with soil (HC) of the in vitro system after three months. ${BARS} ${LET} ${DOSES}`));
add(P('{{c:c25}}Colonization{{/c}} of transformed carrot roots ranged between 83.7 ± 1.6 and 91.1 ± 2.2% (%F) and between 40.5 ± 4.2 and 49.6 ± 4.7% (%I), with arbuscules between 59.5 ± 26.6 and 74.2 ± 3.7%. {{c:tukey}}There were no significant differences among Cu levels in any colonization parameter{{/c}}, although vesicles (59.1, 28.2 and 36.3%) and intraradical spores (80.7, 34.4 and 57.1%) tended to be less abundant with Cu (Fig. 3).'));
add(IMG('Fig3_colonizacion_in_vitro.png', 605, 202));
