add(H1('Discussion'));
add(P('The present study evaluated the effect of fungal strain GC3 on plants treated with a heavy metal, using techniques such as microPIXE to locate the distribution of elements in fungal structures and roots.'));
add(P('In our study, the *R. intraradices* GC3 strain, isolated from an uncontaminated grassland, {{c:c35}}did not affect the growth of inoculated plants{{/c}} under the conditions studied. After adding {{c:c36}}high concentrations of Cu{{/c}}, sunflower plants tolerated the stress without negative effects on growth and biomass, ==demonstrating their potential use in restoration projects as a phytoremediation plant.=='));
add(P('The addition of Cu did not affect the intensity of colonization by GC3 in sunflower, showing high tolerance to this contaminant. The ERM developed in the rhizosphere was not affected either, although a tendency to decrease with increasing Cu was observed.'));
add(P('{{c:claimD}}In the treatments representing stress due to high Cu concentrations, GRSP detection was greater in cultures inoculated with GC3 than in controls.{{/c}} The highest T-GRSP concentration was recorded with the highest Cu dose. ==Therefore, it can be inferred that the increase in GRSP detection could be due to an AM fungal response to environmental stress.== AM fungi have been found in environments with very high levels of heavy metals, so they are thought to have developed strategies to avoid metal damage (Ferrol et al. 2009). GRSP, due to its binding capacity, ==can capture metals by fixing them in its complex structure and preventing damage to cellular metabolism== (González-Chávez et al. 2004; Cornejo et al. 2008).'));
add(P('Previous studies agree that under adverse conditions GRSP production by AM fungi increases while mycelial growth decreases (Rillig and Steinberg 2002; Hammer and Rillig 2011). Enkhtuya et al. (2000) found ERM growth to be a highly sensitive indicator of stress effects in their experiment.'));
add(P('Regarding the distribution of Cu and P, the in vivo results show that plants translocated P to the shoot in the absence of Cu, {{c:c37}}==whereas they retained P in the root in the presence of the metal==.{{/c}} Feil et al. (2020) showed in *Cucumis sativus* that, at high Cu concentrations (above 25 µM), shoot and root growth were stunted and the P influx rate diminished. Abd_Allah et al. (2015) found that cadmium-stressed sunflower plants showed reduced P uptake, whereas plants inoculated with AM fungi not only increased P uptake but also ameliorated the deleterious effect of cadmium. This did not occur in our case.'));
add(P('{{c:frag}}To discern whether P was distributed in the root or in fungal structures in the in vitro assay, the microPIXE technique was used.{{/c}} Calcium and potassium were efficient choices to delimit each compartment evaluated: mycelium, roots and vesicles. P was distributed mainly in the ERM and vesicles, whereas Cu was located in the vesicles.'));
add(H1('{{c:concl}}Conclusion{{/c}}'));
